  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged period of dry and quiet weather through \nnext week with cold nights and mild afternoons, especially Tuesday \nand beyond. \n\nThough the surface low will continue to move well east of the region \ntoday, the mid level trough is lagging behind leading to continued \ncyclonic flow with a cold pool aloft. Though lacking enough moisture \nfor appreciable precipitation (PWATs near 0.3\") we'll see plenty of \ndiurnal cloudcover popping up by late morning/early afternoon. The \nexception will be over the high terrain of the Berkshires where the \naddition of upslope orographic lift may be enough to overcome \nlacking moisture and produce some isolated showers in the afternoon. \n\nLooking toward the latter half of the weekend and next week we see \ngood consensus amongst the guidance in a prolonged stretch dominated \nby a very broad area of high pressure over the entire eastern half \nof the U.S. Besides lack of moisture (expect plenty of sun with no \nrain chances) this places SNE under dry westerly flow leading to \nclear, calm nights and good radiational cooling. Thus, while \nafternoons will feature milder than average highs in the 60s by \nWednesday and beyond, big temperature swings will be common between \nearly morning and mid afternoon.",
